# FMT_engrafter sample manifest
#
# One row per sequencing sample. This table drives every comparison in stage 4 —
# without it you have a genome-by-sample matrix with no way to interpret it.
#
# Columns
#   sample_id    Must match the FASTQ basename used by OpusTaxa (e.g. Donor_01
#                for Donor_01_R1_001.fastq.gz). This is the join key for every
#                downstream table.
#   subject_id   Groups samples from the same person. Recipient timepoints share
#                a subject_id; the donor has their own.
#   sample_type  donor | recipient
#   timepoint    NA for donor material. For recipients use an ordered, zero-padded
#                label: Day00 (pre-FMT), Day07, Day28, ...
#   donor_id     For recipient rows: which donor they received. Blank for donors.
#                Required — it is what lets you check donor MAGs against the
#                correct recipient rather than against the whole cohort.
#
# Notes
#   - Day00 means the PRE-FMT baseline. Every recipient needs one; engraftment
#     cannot be distinguished from persistence without it.
#   - The donor sample must be the material actually administered, not a separate
#     donation from another date.
#   - Keep timepoint labels sortable as strings (Day07, not Day7) so plots order
#     correctly without extra parsing.

sample_id	subject_id	sample_type	timepoint	donor_id
Donor_01	donor01	donor	NA
Donor_02	donor02	donor	NA
Donor_03	donor03	donor	NA
Recipient_01_Day00	recip01	recipient	Day00	donor01
Recipient_01_Day07	recip01	recipient	Day07	donor01
Recipient_01_Day28	recip01	recipient	Day28	donor01
Recipient_02_Day00	recip02	recipient	Day00	donor02
Recipient_02_Day07	recip02	recipient	Day07	donor02
Recipient_02_Day28	recip02	recipient	Day28	donor02
Recipient_03_Day00	recip03	recipient	Day00	donor03
Recipient_03_Day07	recip03	recipient	Day07	donor03
Recipient_03_Day28	recip03	recipient	Day28	donor03
